        <body>
            <div xml:id="d139">

                <pb facs="0397" n="369" xml:id="pg369"/>

                <head>139. Message From the Secretary of State to the President<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d139-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1955-12-17"><hi rendition="#italic">December 17,
                        1955</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. President</hi>: My talks here Saturday with
                    von Brentano, Spaak and Monnet<anchor n="2" xml:id="d139-f2"/> all show that the
                    British are working hard to block the development of the European idea in terms
                    of atomic energy and a common market. This is somewhat in the same pattern as
                    their early opposition to EDC. I feel that this is a matter which we will have
                    to discuss seriously with Eden and Macmillan when they come to Washington.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Foster</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d139-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d139-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 110.11–DU/12–1755. Secret.
                    Transmitted in Dulte 5 from Paris, 5 p.m., and marked “eyes only Acting
                    Secretary for President from Secretary.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d139-f2" n="2" xml:id="d139-f2-content">
                <p>The conversations with von Brentano and Spaak are summarized in Secto 19, <ref
                        target="#d140"><hi rendition="#italic">infra</hi></ref>, and Secto 22, <ref
                        target="#d141">Document 141</ref>; a memorandum of the conversation with
                    Monnet is printed <ref target="#d140"><hi rendition="#italic"
                    >supra</hi></ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
